}]]}],["$","div",null,{"className":"article-body","children":[[["$","p","0-[Untitled Sans](https://klim.co.nz/fonts/untitled-sans/) began as an attempt to make another typeface plainer.",{"className":"$undefined","children":[["$","a","0",{"href":"https://klim.co.nz/fonts/untitled-sans/","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Untitled Sans"]}]," began as an attempt to make another typeface plainer."]}],["$","p","1-Kris Sowersby removed some of the more noticeable details, lost the thread, put the project away, returned to it years later, and nearly threw it out. It was only after reading about [Super Normal](https://jaspermorrison.com/publications/essays/super-normal) that he found a way to understand what he’d been making: maybe being deliberately ordinary was enough of a reason for a typeface to exist.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["Kris Sowersby removed some of the more noticeable details, lost the thread, put the project away, returned to it years later, and nearly threw it out. It was only after reading about ",["$","a","1",{"href":"https://jaspermorrison.com/publications/essays/super-normal","rel":"noreferrer","children":["Super Normal"]}]," that he found a way to understand what he’d been making: maybe being deliberately ordinary was enough of a reason for a typeface to exist."]}],["$","p","2-I love that the explanation arrived late. That feels closer to how making usually works: the work moves before the story catches up.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["I love that the explanation arrived late. That feels closer to how making usually works: the work moves before the story catches up."]}],["$","p","3-Sowersby describes deliberately [removing traces](https://klim.co.nz/blog/untitled-sans-serif-design-information/) of his own recognizable style from the letters, which sounds almost perverse to my designer brain. I'm encouraged to make things distinctive, ownable, memorable—something that can stand up in a presentation and explain why it deserves to exist.",{"className":"$undefined","children":["Sowersby describes deliberately ","$Lf"," of his own recognizable style from the letters, which sounds almost perverse to my designer brain. I'm encouraged to make things distinctive, ownable, memorable—something that can stand up in a presentation and explain why it deserves to exist."]}],"$L10","$L11","$L12","$L13"],"$L14","$L15"]}],"$L16","$L17"]}]]}]]}]
